  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n- **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" covers `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It has about 45 fields, including `response` and `response.keyword`, and its time field is `timestamp`. The sample data also has an `@timestamp` field, but that isn't the configured time field.\n- **Dashboard:** \"S4-ca89125b Web logs\" (id `154869b5-ddb9-4774-910b-6c8424c94aa3`) has two panels. \"Requests over time\" is a line chart of request counts by `timestamp`. The second is a bar chart of request counts by `response.keyword`.\n- **Rule:** \"S4-ca89125b 5xx errors\" (id `0cc79999-d962-436c-bc44-42d8c37bab2c`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute, counts documents from the last 5 minutes, and fires when the count is above 10. The query matches `response.keyword` from \"500\" to \"599\". That is a string range, which works because every code has three digits. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it afterwards.\n- **Panel edit:** I renamed the response-code panel to \"Responses by code\". A read-back of the dashboard shows both panel titles.\n- **Rule read-back:** The rule reads back as enabled with a 1m schedule.\n\nI didn't run the rule against the data, so I haven't seen it fire.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
